Given a binary Tree, find its vertical traversal starting from the leftmost level to the rightmost level. Note: If multiple nodes are at the same horizontal distance from the root and on the same level, they should be printed in the order they appear in a level-order traversal (top-to-bottom, left-to-right).Here, Horizontal distance is calculated from the root to a specific node by counting how many times we move left or right along the unique path from the root to that node.Horizontal Distance = Number of right moves − Number of left moves in the path from the root to that node.Examples:  Input: root = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, N, N, N, 8, N, 9, N, 10, 11, N] Output: [[4], [2], [1, 5, 6, 11], [3, 8, 9], [7], [10]]Explanation: The below image shows the horizontal distances used to print vertical traversal starting from the leftmost level to the rightmost level. Input: root = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, N, 6] Output: [[4], [2], [1, 5], [3], [6]]Explanation: From left to right the vertical order will be [[4], [2], [1, 5], [3], [6]]Try It YourselfTable of Content[Naive Approach] Traversing the Tree for Each Vertical Line - O(n ^ 2) Time and O(n) Space[Better Approach] DFS and Sorting – O(n log n) Time and O(n) Space[Expected Approach] BFS – O(n) Time and O(n) Space[Naive Approach] Traversing the Tree for Each Vertical Line - O(n ^ 2) Time and O(n) SpaceThe idea is to first traverse the tree to find the minimum and maximum horizontal distances (HD) from the root. In the example tree, the minimum HD is -2 (node 4) and the maximum HD is 3 (node 10).Working of Approach:Find the minimum and maximum horizontal distances (HD) from the root.Iterate through each vertical line from the minimum HD to the maximum HD.Perform a level-order traversal (BFS) and collect nodes whose HD matches the current vertical line.Store the collected nodes in the result in left-to-right vertical order.
